WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:07.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  welcome back to cbs this morning  the body of the late congressman john  lewis will arrive on capitol hill later  today  where he will lie in state this week  yesterday lewis made  one final journey just one across the  historic edmond pettis bridge in selma  alabama  his flag draped casket was carried on a  horse-drawn wagon  cbs this morning saturday co-host  michelle miller has been in alabama to  witness a very emotional send-off  she joins us from the statehouse in  montgomery michelle i watched all  weekend saturday and sunday it was so  tender and so touching and so  well deserved good morning to you  good morning indeed it was the 80 year  old civil rights icon was honored  certainly remembered yesterday and that  casket  carrying his body arrived here at the  alabama state capitol  here in montgomery where the public paid  its respects  it was especially poignant because this  was the intended end of  end of the 1965 march for voting rights  and that peaceful march as you well know  was  ended or ended rather in what has become  known  as bloody sunday  two horses pulled the flag draped casket  of civil rights leader john lewis  through the streets of selma  as a crowd gathered to watch some  chanting his motto  good trouble as he was carried across  the alabama river  one last time my life  is better selma is better  this nation and this world are better  because of john robert lewis  congresswoman terry sewell led sunday's  prayer service  she called lewis a friend a mentor and a  hero  this is personal for you very personal  to walk in the footsteps of john lewis  with john lewis  that's a treat of a lifetime and i got  to do it over and over again  cheyenne webb kreisberg was at the foot  of the bridge sunday  she'd been there before with john lewis  in 1965 cheyenne was just eight years  old  the youngest marcher that day she too  was met with  tear gas by alabama state troopers what  does it mean for you to be here today  when i look at what happened on the  bloody sunday march  in the 60s people were beaten by the  troopers  and the policemen but today  we will see congressman lewis take his  last march  and he's being embraced he's being  escorted by them  and he deserves that and the picture of  bloody sunday  has never left my heart neither my mind  people who never met lewis were also  compelled to be here  like the bulgers who wanted their little  girls to witness history  they said goodbye as state troopers  saluted the man their predecessors had  attacked  and then as three-year-old madison  bulger struggled for a view  state trooper bubba bell swept her up  into his shoulders  validation perhaps of john lewis's long  hard fight that was a sign  that we're heading in headed in the  right direction and it was a sign to him  that his work was not in vain  of course recent events have showed us  that we have yet further to go  but the work the passion the sacrifice  of leaders like  lewis certainly propelled us further  ahead to a better place  a better place than this was in 1965  and 150 years before that when  montgomery was the capital  of the confederacy gail  michelle how lovely that you had  cheyenne to talk to all these years  later  the scene that got me was where the  troopers saluting him when years ago he  had been beaten by the troopers i saw  the services from his family who always  called him robert  and one sister told the story when he  left home to go see dr martin luther  king his mother said  don't get into trouble don't get in the  way and they said we all knew that he  would do something  good trouble from a very good man  yes he was thank you michelle miller  later today we'll bring you live special  coverage right here on cbs on cbsn  on the arrival of the casket caring  congressman john lewis in washington  for public viewing at the u.s capitol

